# PowerDNS Helm Chart

{{ template "chart.badgesSection" . }}

[PowerDNS Authoritative Server](https://doc.powerdns.com/authoritative/) with its REST API, packaged for
Kubernetes by UnxWares, with an optional [PowerDNS Recursor](https://doc.powerdns.com/recursor/).

## Features

- **Authoritative DNS** with DNSSEC, on SQLite (single replica + PVC), PostgreSQL or MySQL/MariaDB (scale out).
- **Automatic schema creation**: the schema shipped in the PowerDNS image is applied when the database is empty (idempotent init container).
- **REST API** for [ExternalDNS](https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/external-dns/), PowerDNS-Admin, cert-manager webhooks... The API key is generated and kept across upgrades, or read from your own Secret.
- **Optional Recursor** that forwards your zones to the authoritative server and resolves everything else (DNSSEC validation on).
- **Hardened by default**: non-root (uid 953), read-only root filesystem, every capability dropped, seccomp `RuntimeDefault`, no service account token. DNS listens on an unprivileged port inside the pod and the Service exposes it on 53.
- **Secrets never land in ConfigMaps**: they are injected as environment variables and rendered at startup into a memory-backed volume.
- **Prometheus** `/metrics` with optional ServiceMonitors, **NetworkPolicy** isolating the API, PodDisruptionBudget, HPA (SQL backends), topology spread.

## Installation

```bash
helm repo add unxwares https://helm.unxwares.studio
helm repo update
helm install dns unxwares/powerdns --namespace dns --create-namespace
```

Charts are signed: see [verifying signatures](https://github.com/UnxWares/helm/blob/main/SECURITY.md#verifying-chart-signatures)
and add `--verify --keyring <keyring>` to `helm install`.

## Examples

### Expose DNS outside the cluster

```yaml
service:
  type: LoadBalancer
  externalTrafficPolicy: Local   # keep client IPs (ACLs, AXFR)
config:
  default-soa-content: ns1.example.com. hostmaster.@ 0 10800 3600 604800 3600
```

### High availability (PostgreSQL backend, 3 replicas)

The authoritative servers are stateless with `gpgsql` or `gmysql`: every replica reads the same database, so the
deployment is as available as that database (use an HA PostgreSQL or MySQL, e.g. CloudNativePG or OpenEverest).

```yaml
backend:
  type: gpgsql
  gpgsql:
    host: pdns-db-rw.databases.svc
    dbname: pdns
    user: pdns
    existingSecret: pdns-db        # key: password
    extraConnectionParameters: "sslmode=require"
replicaCount: 3                    # or autoscaling.enabled: true
podDisruptionBudget:
  enabled: true
recursor:
  replicaCount: 2
  podDisruptionBudget:
    enabled: true
```

What the chart takes care of:

- replicas are spread across nodes, then zones, by default (`spreadAcrossNodes`, never blocks scheduling);
- replicas starting together on an empty database do not fail on the schema creation (one creates it, the others wait);
- the API key is generated once and shared by every replica, so the API answers the same on any of them;
- with several replicas, each server refreshes its list of zones every 30 s instead of 300 s
  (`zoneCacheRefreshInterval`).

What to know:

- a **zone** created through the API is served at once by the replica that received the call, and by the others
  after their next zone cache refresh, plus the packet cache: about a minute with the defaults. Until then they
  answer REFUSED for it. Lower `zoneCacheRefreshInterval`, or set it to `0` to disable the zone cache (one database
  query per uncached question), if new zones must be served everywhere at once;
- **records** changed in an existing zone are seen by every replica after their caches expire: 20 s
  (`cache-ttl`, `query-cache-ttl`), 60 s for a name that did not exist (`negquery-cache-ttl`). Tune them in `config`;
- the `gsqlite3` backend is single-replica only.

### ExternalDNS

```yaml
# ExternalDNS values
provider:
  name: pdns
extraArgs:
  - --pdns-server=http://dns-powerdns-api.dns.svc:8081
env:
  - name: EXTERNAL_DNS_PDNS_API_KEY
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: dns-powerdns
        key: api-key
```

Allow ExternalDNS through the NetworkPolicy:

```yaml
networkPolicy:
  enabled: true
  apiAllowedFrom:
    - namespaceSelector:
        matchLabels:
          kubernetes.io/metadata.name: external-dns
```

### Recursor for the cluster

```yaml
recursor:
  enabled: true
  authoritativeZones:
    - example.com
    - 10.in-addr.arpa
  # forwardZonesRecurse: ["9.9.9.9:53"]   # use an upstream resolver instead of the root servers
```

### Managing zones

Zones created through the API are served immediately by the replica that received the call (by the others after
their next zone cache refresh, see above). With `pdnsutil`, ask the server to refresh its zone cache; with several
replicas, the others pick the zone up at their next refresh:

```bash
kubectl exec -n dns deploy/dns-powerdns -c pdns -- \
  sh -c 'pdnsutil zone create example.com ns1.example.com && pdns_control rediscover'
```

## Notes

- **GitOps**: when the API key is generated, tools that render templates offline (Argo CD...) cannot read it back and would rotate it on every sync. Set `api.key` or `api.existingSecret`.
- **gsqlite3** supports a single replica; the chart refuses `replicaCount > 1` or the HPA with it. Use `gpgsql` or
  `gmysql` for high availability.
- **IPv6**: remove `[::]` from `dns.listenAddresses` (and `::` from `recursor.listenAddresses`) if your pods have IPv6 disabled.
- **Images**: a default install runs `docker.io/powerdns/pdns-auth-51` only. `docker.io/powerdns/pdns-recursor-54`
  (`recursor.enabled`) and the PostgreSQL/MySQL client images of the schema init (`backend.schemaInit.postgresImage`,
  `backend.schemaInit.mysqlImage`, for gpgsql/gmysql) are only pulled when enabled; the Artifact Hub security report covers
  the default image.
- `webserver.allowFrom` and `recursor.allowFrom` default to private ranges; add your pod CIDR if it uses another range (e.g. `100.64.0.0/10`).

